{"name":"Hammerhead Shark","hex":"#4e7496","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hammerhead shark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the family Sphyrnidae of sharks.\nAny of various sharks of the genus Sphyrna having the eyes set on projections (cephalofoils) from the sides of the head, which gives it a hammer shape.\nA winghead shark (Eusphyra blochii).","Any of various sharks of the genus Sphyrna having the eyes set on projections (cephalofoils) from the sides of the head, which gives it a hammer shape."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hammerhead_shark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hammerhead shark","blocks":[{"text":"The hammerhead sharks are a group of sharks that form the family Sphyrnidae, named for the unusual and distinctive form of their heads, which are flattened and laterally extended into a cephalofoil. The shark's eyes are placed one on each end of this T-shaped structure, with their small mouths directly centered and underneath. Most hammerhead species are placed in the genus Sphyrna, while the winghead shark is placed in its own genus, Eusphyra. Many different—but not necessarily mutually exclusive—functions have been postulated for the cephalofoil, including sensory reception, manoeuvering, and prey manipulation. The cephalofoil gives the shark superior binocular vision and depth perception, as well as increased surface area for electroreceptors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Scalloped_Hammerhead_Shark_Sphyrna_Lewini_%28226845659%29.jpeg/330px-Scalloped_Hammerhead_Shark_Sphyrna_Lewini_%28226845659%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hammerhead_shark","wikidata":"Q208490"},{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/330px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark","wikidata":"Q7372"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Hammerhead_Atlantis.jpg/500px-Hammerhead_Atlantis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Hammerhead_Atlantis.jpg/1280px-Hammerhead_Atlantis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Hammerhead_Atlantis.jpg/500px-Hammerhead_Atlantis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A hammerhead shark in shallow water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHammerhead_Atlantis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hammerhead_shark.jpg/500px-Hammerhead_shark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hammerhead_shark.jpg/500px-Hammerhead_shark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The hammer-like shape of the head enhances sensory ability – positioning eyes to excel and widely distributing electroreceptors – enabling hammerhead sharks to search for prey more effectively.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHammerhead_shark.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Hammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg/500px-Hammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Hammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg/1280px-Hammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Hammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg/500px-Hammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A great hammerhead gliding along the sea bed","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHammerhead_Shark_-_Marko_Dimitrijevic.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Eusphyra_blochii_csiro-nfc.jpg/330px-Eusphyra_blochii_csiro-nfc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Eusphyra_blochii_csiro-nfc.jpg/330px-Eusphyra_blochii_csiro-nfc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEusphyra_blochii_csiro-nfc.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Sphyrna_corona_SI3.jpg/500px-Sphyrna_corona_SI3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Sphyrna_corona_SI3.jpg/500px-Sphyrna_corona_SI3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail"